Constructing core-shell metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) based on two topologically distinct MOFs is a great way to increase MOF material complexity and explore their new functions. However, such nucleation process is energetically less favored compared to epitaxial growth due to mismatched unit cell parameters. Here, two kinetic factors, nucleation kinetics and dissolution kinetics, are revealed to be two key factors to overcome this challenge. Through kinetic control, we demonstrate the growth of 4 types of Zr/Hf-MOF shells uniformly and contiguously on 7 different core MOFs including ZIF8, an acid labile core. Taking the advantage of the modular synthesis of Zr-MOFs, we demonstrate that post-synthetic covalent surface modification on a non-functionalizable MOF surface can be made possible through core-shell construction. We also demonstrated that the size selective catalytic behavior can be systematically tuned through changing either ligand length or ligand functionality.
## Introduction
Hierarchical arrangement of materials into core-shell architectures is a widely implemented strategy to endow materials with new functions. A widely accepted notation for a core-shell material can be written as $\mathsf{A}@\mathsf{B}$ where B is the shell grown on the core material $\mathsf{A}.^{1-3}$ In many cases, the core possesses the main physical and chemical properties such as localized surface plasmon resonance,4 magnetism,5 photoluminescence,6 and catalytic activity7 etc. whereas the shell is responsible for communicating with surrounding media thereby ensuring the composite material with good dispersity, stability, targeting specificity, and bio-compatibility etc.5, 8-10
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a broad class of porous materials constructed through hierarchical molecular assembly of metal building units and organic linkers. Their high designability paves their way towards many potential applications.11-19 To increase the structural complexity of a MOF, a common way is to combine different functional moieties in a single crystalline phase as exemplified by the concept of “heterogeneity within order”.20-25 Alternatively, hierarchical assembly of MOFs with polymers,26-27 metal nanoparticles,28-29 metal oxides,30 carbon materials,31 covalent organic frameworks (COFs)32 and enzyme33 etc. in a core-shell fashion has been repeatedly demonstrated to further extend the functions and utilities of MOFs to a new level.
Core-shell MOFs are a special group of hierarchical MOF composites as the dense MOF shell can enrich the functions of the resultant composite from at least the following three aspects: 1) discriminate larger guest molecules through a molecular sieving mechanism for selective adsorption or catalysis;34-36 2) serve as a protective layer to mitigate the inherent instability of the core MOF;34 and 3) serve as a transition layer for the covalent attachment of small molecules or polymers to bridge the core MOF and its surroundings.37 Recently, a new concept, domain building blocks (DBBs), put forward by Rosi et al. suggested multilayer core-shell construction can effectively diversify the functions of MOF materials.38 However, the major bottleneck to achieve such complexity is the lack of synthetic tools. For most of the coreshell MOFs reported to date rely on epitaxial growth which greatly constrained the range of material selection.29, 39-44 Recently, Tsung et al. and Li et al. successively reported a surfactant mediated and a random copolymer (RCP) mediated growth of uniform ZIF-8 shell on various core MOFs.45-46 For large single crystals, Zhou et al. unveiled the principles for the construction of MOF-on-MOF structure using retrosynthetic guidelines.47 However, all these examples only allow the growth of shell MOFs with chemically more labile bonds than that of the core MOFs. Using robust Zr MOFs as shell, although highly desired, has only been reported by Zhou et al. through a kinetic controlled one-step synthetic of large PCN-222@UiO-67 crystals48  and by Tan et al. through an Ostwald ripeningmediated growth of UiO-66@PCN-222.49 Yet, the generalizability of this method to other MOFs especially chemically more labile MOFs remain unknown.
Here, we uncover two kinetic factors that are responsible for the construction of uniform and contiguous Zr/Hf-MOF shells on both robust and labile core MOFs. First, we found that boosting the nucleation kinetics by raising the precursor concentration is critical for the rapid formation of densely packed small MOF nuclei around the core MOF.  Continued growth of these nuclei sealed the gaps among crystallites resulting in dense and contiguous Zr/Hf-MOF shells grown on a series of robust core MOFs including MOF-801, MIL-96(Al), MIL101(Cr), UiO-66, MIL-53(Cr) and Ni-MOF-74. To overcome the seemingly impossible task of growing Zr-MOF shells on an acid labile core MOF such as ZIF-8, lowering the dissolution kinetics of the core MOF is key. By reducing the quantity of the Zr-MOF precursors, the dissolution of ZIF-8 was kept at a very low rate thereby allowing the growth of a dense Zr-MOF shell without compromising the integrity of the ZIF-8 core. By using an amine functionalized Zr-MOF shell, covalent surface modification on a non-functionalizable MOF is now possible through core-shell construction. We also demonstrated that size selective catalysis can be precisely tuned by both ligand substitution and functionalization in the shell MOF.
## Results and discussion
Nucleation kinetics governed growth of UiO-66 on MIL-101(Cr)
The nucleation of one MOF on another topologically distinct MOF surface is a heterogeneous nucleation process. According to the classic nucleation theory, the critical energy barrier of a heterogeneous nucleation process equals to the product of the homogeneous nucleation energy barrier and a function of the contact angle (CA) $\theta$ (Eq. 1). Since the CA term is always smaller than 1, heterogeneous nucleation is thereby energetically favored over homogeneous nucleation.50
$$
\Delta G_{h e t e r o g e n e o u s}=\Delta G_{h o m o g e n e o u s}\times\frac{2-3\cos\theta+\cos^{3}\theta}{4}\quad(0\leq\theta<180^{\circ})
$$
Eq. 1
The tendency for this to occur depends on 1) the nucleation kinetics, and 2) the surface properties of the core MOF. However, compared to epitaxial growth between two structurally identical MOF analogues, it is energetically less favorable due to mismatched unit cell parameters. To achieve a uniform defect-free MOF shell through non-epitaxial growth, one strategy is to modify the core MOF surface with “nucleation friendly” small molecules or polymers to lower the nucleation energy barrier as demonstrated by Tsung et al. and Li et al. in their effort to grow a dense ZIF-8 shell on various core MOFs.45- 46 Alternatively, increasing the monomer concentration can simultaneously boost the nucleation kinetics and reduce the critical nuclei size to reach the same goal. To verity this hypothesis, a robust core MOF, MIL-101(Cr), was selected for the attempted growth of UiO-66.
MIL-101(Cr) was synthesized according to the reported method by Zhao et al. with slight modification.51 The crystallinity of the MOF was confirmed by both the transmission electron microscope (TEM) image and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure S1 and S2). The average particle size is $300\pm35$ nm as determined by TEM. To investigate the effect of concentration on the nucleation kinetics of UiO-66, MIL-101(Cr) crystals were immersed in three dimethylformamide (DMF) solutions containing 25, 50, and $100~\mathsf{m M}$ of $Z r C l_{4}$ and terephthalic acid $(H_{2}B D C)$ and heated to ${120^{\circ}}{\mathsf C}$ . After 10 min of growth, a smooth layer of amorphous deposition was observed on the surface of all three MIL-101(Cr) samples as shown in the TEM images (Figure $|\mathfrak{i}_{\mathfrak{r},\vee,\mathfrak{i}\times\mathfrak{l}}$ . Energy dispersion X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping results for Figure ix reveal that this deposition layer contains $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ (Figure S3 and S4). However, no ordering was observed according to the PXRD patterns (Figure S5). Therefore, this layer is likely to be an amorphous $Z r$ -ligand complex (AZC). At $20{\mathsf{m i n}}$ , both the 50 and $100\mathrm{\mM}$ grown samples show roughening on the particle surface due to the nucleation of UiO-66. Their PXRD patterns also confirmed such transformation as evidenced by the emergence of the (111) facet diffraction peak of UiO-66 at $7.38\triangleq$ (Figure 1C). In contrast, the sample grown in $25~\mathsf{m M}$ solution did not start nucleation until $40\min$ , much slower than the other two samples (Figure 1iii and S6). Moreover, the size of the nuclei is also considerably larger leading to a lower surface coverage on MIL-101(Cr). After $\mathsf{1h}$ , the MIL-101(Cr) $@$ UiO-66 core-shell structure formed in the 100 mM solution exhibit well-defined shell thickness and integrity. Comparably, $25~\mathsf{m M}$ solution only resulted in partial coverage of large UiO-66 crystallites on the core MOF. The sample grown in the $50~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}$ solution also shows near full coverage of UiO-66 but the crystallite size is relatively large resulting in a rough surface texture (Figure 1viii). The EDS mapping results of Figure 1xii reveal the expected layered Zr and Cr distribution confirming the core-shell morphology of MIL-101(Cr)@UiO-66 particle (Figure 1B). These results suggest that rapid nucleation is critical for the formation of a dense UiO66 shell on MIL-101(Cr). In addition, despite the rapid nucleation, the shell growth process is relatively slow but steady which allows continuous increase of UiO-66 shell thickness from $\sim13\mathsf{n m}$ at $0.5{\ h}$ to $69\mathsf{n m}$ at 2 h (Figure S7).
## Growth of Zr/Hf-MOF shells on various core MOFs
Next, we extended this strategy to the growth of a series of Zr/Hf-MOF shells on various core MOFs. Figure 2A-H show the TEM images of MOF-801@UiO-66, MIL-96(Al) $@$ UiO-66-NH2, MIL-53(Cr) $@$ UiO-66, MIL-101(Cr) $@$ UiO-66(Hf), MIL101(Cr)@UiO-66-NH2, Ni-MOF-74@UiO-66-NH2, UiO66@MOF-801 and MIL-101(Cr)@MOF-801, respectively. All the samples exhibit dense and uniform shell layers with thickness in the range of 10 to $82{\mathsf{n m}}$ . The PXRD patterns of these core-shell samples all clearly exhibit combinations of two crystalline phases (Figure S8 to S15).
Considering the generalizability of this shell growth method, it is possible to increase the complexity of core-shell MOF composites by adding more DBBs, in another word, growing more than one shell layers. As a proof of principle, a doubleshell composite, $\mathsf{M I L-101}(\mathsf{C r})@\mathsf{U i O-66}(\mathsf{Z r})@\mathsf{M O F-801}(\mathsf{H f})$ was synthesized. The TEM micrograph (Figure 2I) unambiguously shows that a MIL-101(Cr) particle was uniformly coated with a \~40 nm UiO-66 shell wrapped in another ${\sim}30$ nm MOF-801(Hf) shell. Further structural information was provided by the EDS mapping of Cr, Zr, Hf (Figure 2J) and the PXRD pattern (Figure 2K). The unusual spreading of Cr EDS signal across the shell layers is likely due to leaching of Cr from the core under acidic condition. Recent studies by Liu et al. also suggested that despite the well-known robustness of MIL-101(Cr), the center of the MIL-101(Cr) particles is less crystalline and more labile than the exterior and are prone to dissolution.52 Nevertheless, the three-layered morphology MIL-101(Cr)@UiO-66(Zr)@MOF801(Hf) is clearly demonstrated. This example suggests that the DBB concept can be further extended to MOFs with arbitrary unit cell parameter combinations.
In addition to $Z\mathrm{r/Hf-MOFs}$ , this rapid nucleation approach can also be applied to the growth of ZIF-8 shell. At lower precursor concentration, ZIF-8 cannot be grown on UiO-66 surface. By increasing the concentration of 2-methylimidazole (MIM) from 0.576 M to 1.344 M, UiO-66@ZIF-8 core-shell MOF was successfully synthesized (Figure S16).
Growth of a Zr-MOF shell on an acid labile core MOF through dissolution kinetics control
Among these core-shell examples, it is worth noting that NiMOF-74 is regard as a more labile MOF than UiO-66-NH2. Nevertheless, the growth condition of UiO-66- $\cdot N H_{2}$ was mild enough to be tolerated by Ni-MOF-74 nanorods thereby successfully achieving a well-defined core-shell structure. This observation drove us to pursue the possibility of growing ZrMOF shells on more labile core MOFs.
The key challenge for growing Zr-MOFs on an acid labile core MOF is that the core typically cannot withstand the aggressive synthetic condition with the presence of acidic precursors such as $Z r^{4+}$ and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ thus leading to dissolution. Such phenomenon has been observed by Zhong et al. in the case of growing UiO-66 on MOF-5.53 During our early attempt to grow UiO-66- $\cdot N H_{2}$ on ZIF-8 using rapid nucleation approach, we found that UiO-66-NH2 crystallites nucleated around ZIF-8 crystallites to form a continuous hollow capsule (Figure 3Bi) whereas ZIF-8 served as a sacrificial template and was completely etched away by acidic precursors. Interestingly, when large ZIF-8 particles with a size of $1.0{\pm}0.1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were used as the core, ZIF-8 residuals were observed in the hollow UiO-66- $\cdot N H_{2}$ shells resembling a yolk-shell structure (Figure S17). This implicates that by manipulating the dissolution kinetics of ZIF-8, it is possible to grow a Zr-MOF shell without significant etching on the ZIF-8 core. Therefore, we gradually reduced the ZIF-8/UiO-66-NH2 growth ratio54 from 1:1 to 1:2, 1:4, 1:6, 1:8 and 1:10 while keeping the precursor concentration at $100\mathrm{~mM}$ to maintain rapid nucleation kinetics (Figure S18). After $2h$ growth, there is a clear trend suggesting that less growth solution results in slower ZIF-8 dissolution. In particular, at 1:1 growth ratio, no apparent dissolution of ZIF-8 was observed. Meanwhile, a uniform layer of deposition appeared on ZIF-8. EDS mapping results show that Zr is presence in this layer suggesting the formation of an AZC layer similar to that of MIL-101(Cr) at early growth stage (Figure 1Aix). After digesting ZIF-8 using acetic acid, a ${\sim}5\ \mathsf{n m}\ A Z C$ layer was revealed in the form of a slightly collapsed hollow cube. Repeating this growth step one more time with fresh growth solution led to a $Z|\mathsf{F}{-}8@\mathsf{U i O}{-}66{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ core-shell structure containing a ${\sim}15\ \mathsf{n m}\ \mathsf{U i O{-}}66{\-}\mathsf{N H_{2}}$ crystalline shell uniformly coated on the ZIF-8 core. After digesting ZIF-8 core using acid, the hollow polycrystalline UiO- $66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ shell exhibits good structural integrity according to both the PXRD pattern and TEM image (Figure 3C and 3Biii inset). The ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of $Z|{\mathsf{F}}{-}8@{\mathsf{U i O}}{-}66{-}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ falls in between that of ZIF-8 and ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-}}66{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ indicating that the crystallinity and porosity of both MOFs are well maintained (Figure 3D).
To understand the dissolution kinetics, the supernatants of the growth mixtures were taken out at different time points and $Z n^{2+}$ concentration was monitored by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES). Figure 3E shows that complete ZIF-8 dissolution was observed in the 1:10 growth solution after 10 min of reaction. In contrast, only $\sim11\%$ ZIF-8 was dissolved and leached out for the 1:1 growth sample. With the reaction time extended to $3h$ , no further ZIF-8 dissolution was observed. It is clear that by reducing the amount of Zr-MOF precursors, there are less acidic species available in the solution thereby lowering the dissolution kinetics of ZIF-8 at the early stage of shell growth. The rapid deposition of an AZC layer further passivated ZIF-8 surface therefore effectively suppressed the diffusion of acidic species toward ZIF-8 as well as the dissolved species toward the solution, consequently ceasing the dissolution of ZIF-8.
With the amine functionalized $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ as shell, it is now possible to covalently attach small molecules to the core-shell composite for the surface modification of ZIF-8. As a proof of concept, $Z|\mathsf{F}{-}8@\mathsf{U i O}{-}66{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ was reacted with stearoyl chloride in tetrahydrofuran (THF). The extraction experiment shows that alkane modified $Z|\mathsf{F}{-}8@\mathsf{U i O}{-}66{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ can be extracted to the ethyl acetate phase whereas the non-functionalized ZIF$8@{\mathsf{U i O}}-66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ prefers to stay in the aqueous phase (Figure S19). Furthermore, the water contact angle of ZIF- $3@$ UiO-66- $N H_{2}$ increased from $20~\pm~1^{\circ}$ to $135\pm3^{\circ}$ after surface modification suggesting that $Z|{\mathsf{F}}{-}8@{\mathsf{U i O}}{-}66{-}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ surface was successfully modified by hydrophobic alkyl molecules (Figure 3F).
## Manipulating the MOF shell for size selective catalysis
To manifest the tunability of Zr-MOFs, we constructed a series of Pt NPs-containing core-shell catalysts to showcase their ability for size selective hydrogenation through changing either ligand length or ligand functionality. On the other hand, size selective catalysis is also a great way to verify the uniformity of the as-grown shell layers. To start with, ${\sim}2$ nm polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) coated platinum nanoparticles (Pt NPs) were synthesized according to a reported method55 (Figure S20). They were then adsorbed on to the core MOF MIL-101(Cr) or UiO-66 through electrostatic interaction to give MIL$101(\mathsf{C r})@\mathsf{P t}$ (1) and UiO-66@Pt (Figure 4B, S21). Then, UiO-66, UiO-66- $\cdot N H_{2}$ and MOF-801 shells were grown on to ${\mathsf{M O F@P t}}$ to give three core-shell composite catalysts: MIL101(Cr)@Pt@UiO-66, $\mathsf{M I L-101}(\mathsf{C r})@\mathsf{P t}@\mathsf{U i O-66-N H_{2,}}$ , and UiO$66@\mathsf{P t@M O F-801}$ . For convenience, they are denoted as 2, 3, and 4, respectively. The TEM images show the sandwiched structural hierarchy where Pt NPs are located at the core-shell interface. Three nitro compounds, nitrobenzene (NB), 2,3- dimethyl nitrobenzene DMNB), and 1-nitronaphthalene (NN) with increasing molecular sizes were selected to elucidate the size selectivity of the catalysts. After 150 min hydrogenation reaction in ${\sf H}_{2}$ atmosphere, $100\%$ conversion of all three nitro compounds were reached with catalyst 1. With a $30\pm2$ nm UiO66 coating, 2 successfully accomplished full conversion for the smaller NB and DMNB, but only managed to convert $62\%$ NN indicating a size selective behavior. With UiO-66-NH2 and MOF801 coatings, both 3 and 4 exhibit appreciable $(\sim50\%)$
conversion for NB yet very little conversion $(\sim4\%)$ for NN indicating a clear size cutoff for the larger species due to the shortening of linker length (MOF-801) or the introduction of steric functional group $(\mathsf{U i O}-66-\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ . Interestingly, despite the similar reactivity of 3 and 4 towards NB and NN, 4 exhibits much higher conversion $(26\%)$ for DMNB than that of 3 $(6.4\%)$ . This demonstrates that the size selective catalytic behavior can be further fine-tuned through incorporation of steric functional groups such as $-N H_{2}$ . Given the availability of various functional groups in $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ -MOFs and synthetic tools such as post-synthetic modification and ligand exchange etc., it is reasonable to believe molecular recognition in catalysis can be potentially achieved through core-shell construction.
## Conclusions
In summary, we unveiled two key kinetic factors that govern the growth of uniform Zr/Hf-MOF shells on a series of core MOFs. The first kinetic factor, nucleation kinetics, controls the nuclei size of the shell MOF, consequently its surface coverage and uniformity on the core MOF. The second kinetic factor, dissolution kinetics, dictates how fast the core MOF dissolves during the growth process which is a non-negligible factor when an acid labile core MOFs such as ZIF-8 is involved. We believe these principles for constructing core-shell MOFs will guide the rational design of more sophisticated hierarchical MOF composite materials for various applications.
